<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-ascorbate biosynthesis VII (plants, D-galacturonate pathway)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: ASCORBATE| (ascorbic acid, vitamin C) is a reducing agent and antioxidant biosynthesized by plants, eukaryotic algae, some bacteria and many, but not all vertebrates.|FRAME: ASCORBATE| is an essential vitamin in the diet of humans, non-human primates and certain other vertebrates that cannot biosynthesize it. These species rely on mechanisms for efficient uptake and recycling of this vitamin (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-6370|). A deficiency of vitamin C leads to the disorder scurvy. |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is used in the pharmaceutical industry for the production of vitamin supplements, cosmetics and therapeutic preparations. It is also used in the food, beverage and animal feed industries. Reviewed in |CITS: [16516325] [17222174]|. In photosynthesizing plants and eukaryotic algae |FRAME: ASCORBATE| has roles in metabolism, electron transport, the cell cycle and responses to pathogens and abiotic stress. When coupled with its redox system, the ascorbate/glutathione cycle, |FRAME: ASCORBATE| can effectively regulate cellular |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| levels (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-2261|) |CITS: [18460785]|. Interestingly, |FRAME: TAX-1117| do not possess any of the known enzymes for ascorbate biosynthesis, and minimize photo-oxidative stress using alternative mechanisms, such as peroxiredoxins, catalases and glutathione peroxidases, suggesting that the use of ascorbate evolved after the endosymbiotic acquisition of a cyanobacterial ancestor by a non-photosynthetic eukaryote |CITS: [23109712][25768426]|. &lt;B&gt;Ascorbate Biosynthesis in Plants&lt;/B&gt; Four different pathways for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| have been described in plants. &amp;bull; The first plant pathway was suggested in 1998 by Wheeler et al |CITS: [9620799]|. The pathway starts from |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE| and proceeds via the key intermediates |FRAME: L-Galactopyranose| and |FRAME: CPD-330| (see |FRAME: PWY-882|). &amp;bull; A second pathway, known as the L-gulose pathway, also starts with |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE|. However, that pathway proceeds via the key intermediates |FRAME: L-gulopyranose| and |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS:[12954627]| (see |FRAME: PWY4FS-11|). &amp;bull; A third pathway was described from |FRAME: TAX-3747| (strawberry). In that pathway |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is formed from |FRAME: CPD-330|, as in the first pathway. However, the pathway starts from |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE|, and proceeds via |FRAME: CPD0-1083| |CITS:[13363799][12524550]|. Tracer and feeding studies have also shown conversion of |FRAME: CPD-23305| to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| |CITS: [Loewus63]|. (see |FRAME: PWY-8143|). &amp;bull; A fourth pathway suggested that |FRAME: MYO-INOSITOL| can also be channeled towards the production of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| via |FRAME: EC-1.13.99.1|, based on the effect of overexpressing the |FRAME: AT4G26260| gene. According to that proposal, |FRAME: GLUCURONATE| is reduced to |FRAME: L-GULONATE|, which is converted to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| via |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS: [14976233]| (see |FRAME: PWY-8142|). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Back in 1953 Isherwood et al described conversion of |FRAME: CPD-23305| to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| in |FRAME: TAX-3702| |CITS: [13036892][13126085]|. Radiotracer |CITS: [14466265]| and biochemical evidence |CITS: [13363799]| suggested the enzymatic reduction of derivatives of |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE| to |FRAME: CPD0-1083| by plant extracts. A 1999 study that looked at the biosynthesis of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| in |FRAME: TAX-3702| by incubation with a range of potential biosynthetic precursors, analogs, and inhibitors confirmed that administration of |FRAME: CPD-23305| to cultured cells substantially increases their |FRAME: ASCORBATE| content |CITS: [10517845]|. Additional evidence for the existence of this pathway came with the demonstration that overexpression of |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.365|, from |FRAME: TAX-3747| (strawberry) in |FRAME: TAX-3702| plants results in increased levels of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| |CITS: [12524550]|. This pathway utilizes sugars provided by the breakdown of cell walls to form |FRAME: ASCORBATE|. The degradation of pectin releases |FRAME: CPD-23305|, which is converted into |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE| by methyl esterase. This is supported by the observation that the high |FRAME: ASCORBATE| content found in tomato introgression lines IL124 compared with the parental M82 seems to be due to a higher expression of a pectinesterase and two polygalacturonases |CITS: [20691085]|. This pathway seems to be active mainly during fruit ripening in some species. In tomato, |FRAME: ASCORBATE| synthesis in immature green fruit is enhanced only by the supply of |FRAME: L-Galactose|, whereas in red ripened fruits by feeding with either L-galactose and |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE| |CITS: [21984649]|.</Description>
